  "confidences": {"U": 0.9, "A": 0.95, "C": 0.8, "V": 0.98},
  "rationales": {"U":"'Having these requirements unfulﬁlled must not create dan- gers to the system' uses vague nominalization 'dan- gers' and lacks subject reference — it is unclear which system (target system? runtime environment? safety-critical subsystem?) and what constitutes 'danger' (failure mode? hazard? violation of safety integrity level?). This impairs immediate comprehension by stakeholders without domain context.","A":"The phrase 'must not create dan- gers' is syntactically fragmented ('dan- gers' is a line-broken typo), semantically imprecise (no definition of 'danger'), and normatively weak — 'must not create' conflates prohibition with absence of effect; it fails to distinguish between hazard exposure, risk escalation, or violation of safety constraints. The term 'these requirements' is deictic and undefined in isolation.","C":"The statement expresses a safety intent but lacks traceability to recognized safety standards (e.g., ISO 26262 ASIL, IEC 61508 SIL) or system-level hazard analysis outputs; 'dan- gers' is not aligned with formal hazard classification (e.g., 'hazardous event', 'harm', 'unsafe state'). While broadly plausible as a safety goal, it is underspecified for correctness verification against system architecture.","V":"No testable condition or observable behavior is defined: there is no measurable criterion (e.g., 'system shall remain in safe state', 'no fault propagation to Class B components', 'MTTF > 1e6 hours') nor pass/fail boundary. 'Must not create dan- gers' cannot be validated via test, inspection, or analysis — it is an unverifiable safety aphorism."},
  "suggestions": {"U":"The system shall ensure that failure to satisfy any requirement in this specification does not lead to a hazardous event as defined in clause 3.2 of the Safety Case Document SC-2024-001.","A":"For every requirement R in this specification, if R is violated during operation, the system shall transition to and maintain a safe state within 100 ms, as defined by ISO 26262-2:2018 Annex C.","C":"The system shall comply with ASIL-B requirements per ISO 26262-6:2018, such that violation of any functional requirement shall not result in a hazardous event classified as ASIL-B or higher.","V":"When any requirement in this specification is violated under nominal environmental conditions, the system shall activate its Safe State Manager (SSM) and output diagnostic code 'SSM-01' on CAN bus ID 0x1FF within ≤100 ms, verifiable via hardware-in-the-loop test TC-SSM-01."},
  "issues": [
    {"type":"U","evidence":"Having these requirements unfulﬁlled must not create dan- gers to the system.","rewrite":"The system shall ensure that failure to satisfy any requirement in this specification does not lead to a hazardous event as defined in clause 3.2 of the Safety Case Document SC-2024-001."},
    {"type":"A","evidence":"Having these requirements unfulﬁlled must not create dan- gers to the system.","rewrite":"For every requirement R in this specification, if R is violated during operation, the system shall transition to and maintain a safe state within 100 ms, as defined by ISO 26262-2:2018 Annex C."},
    {"type":"V","evidence":"Having these requirements unfulﬁlled must not create dan- gers to the system.","rewrite":"When any requirement in this specification is violated under nominal environmental conditions, the system shall activate its Safe State Manager (SSM) and output diagnostic code 'SSM-01' on CAN bus ID 0x1FF within ≤100 ms, verifiable via hardware-in-the-loop test TC-SSM-01."}
  ]
}
